+
+## Appendix A: Model Equations and Forms
+
+This appendix states the mathematical form of every model available in the platform, in the general notation `y → f(x)`. It is intended as a reference for the methods section of a paper. Each entry gives the model equation and a one-line plain-language reading.
+
+### A.0 Common notation
+
+| Symbol | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `yᵢ` | The dependent variable (outcome) for observation *i* |
+| `xᵢ = (xᵢ₁, …, x_ik)` | The vector of *k* predictors for observation *i* |
+| `β = (β₀, β₁, …, βₖ)` | Coefficients (β₀ is the intercept/constant) |
+| `εᵢ` | The error (residual) term |
+| `λ` | Regularization strength (penalty) for Ridge/LASSO |
+| `L` | Lag operator, where `L·yₜ = yₜ₋₁` |
+| `t` | A time index (time-series and panel models) |
+| `i, t` | Subject *i* at time *t* (panel models) |
+
+The compact matrix form of a linear model is `y = Xβ + ε`.
+
+---
+
+### A.1 Cross-Sectional Models
+
+**Ordinary Least Squares (OLS)**
+
+    yᵢ = β₀ + β₁xᵢ₁ + β₂xᵢ₂ + … + βₖxᵢₖ + εᵢ
+
+Estimated by minimizing the sum of squared errors, `min Σ (yᵢ − xᵢ′β)²`, giving `β̂ = (X′X)⁻¹X′y`.
+*Reading:* a straight-line relationship between predictors and a continuous outcome.
+
+**Generalized Least Squares (GLS)**
+
+    y = Xβ + ε,   with   Var(ε) = Ω
+    β̂ = (X′Ω⁻¹X)⁻¹ X′Ω⁻¹y
+
+*Reading:* like OLS, but corrects for errors that have unequal variance or are correlated across observations.
+
+**Logit (Logistic Regression)**
+
+    P(yᵢ = 1 | xᵢ) = 1 / (1 + e^−(β₀ + β₁xᵢ₁ + … + βₖxᵢₖ))
+
+Equivalently, in log-odds form:  `ln( p / (1 − p) ) = β₀ + β₁xᵢ₁ + … + βₖxᵢₖ`
+*Reading:* models the probability of a yes/no (1/0) outcome; coefficients act on the log-odds.
+
+**Ridge Regression**
+
+    min  Σ (yᵢ − xᵢ′β)²  +  λ Σ βⱼ²
+
+*Reading:* OLS plus an L2 penalty that shrinks coefficients toward zero to stabilize estimates when predictors are correlated.
+
+**LASSO Regression**
+
+    min  Σ (yᵢ − xᵢ′β)²  +  λ Σ |βⱼ|
+
+*Reading:* OLS plus an L1 penalty that can shrink some coefficients exactly to zero, performing automatic variable selection.
+
+**Random Forest**
+
+    ŷ = (1/B) Σ_{b=1}^{B} Tᵦ(x)
+
+Each `Tᵦ` is a regression tree grown on a bootstrap sample using a random subset of predictors at each split.
+*Reading:* the average prediction of many de-correlated trees; captures non-linearities and interactions.
+
+**Bagging (Bootstrap Aggregating)**
+
+    ŷ = (1/B) Σ_{b=1}^{B} Tᵦ(x)
+
+Like Random Forest, but each tree may consider all predictors at every split.
+*Reading:* averages many trees fit on bootstrap resamples to reduce variance.
+
+**Gradient Boosting**
+
+    F₀(x) = initial guess
+    Fₘ(x) = Fₘ₋₁(x) + ν · hₘ(x),   for m = 1, …, M
+    ŷ = F_M(x)
+
+Each `hₘ` is a small tree fit to the negative gradient (the residuals) of the previous stage; `ν` is the learning rate.
+*Reading:* builds the model in stages, each stage correcting the errors of the one before.
+
+---
+
+### A.2 Time-Series Models
+
+**ARIMA / ARIMAX** — as implemented, an ARMA(1,0,1) model with optional exogenous regressors (SARIMAX, order = (1, 0, 1)):
+
+    yₜ = c + β′xₜ + φ₁ yₜ₋₁ + θ₁ εₜ₋₁ + εₜ
+
+The general ARIMA(p, d, q) form is:  `φ(L)(1 − L)ᵈ yₜ = θ(L) εₜ`
+*Reading:* the next value depends on its own recent value (`φ₁ yₜ₋₁`), the recent shock (`θ₁ εₜ₋₁`), and — if supplied — outside predictors `xₜ`.
+
+**Time-Series LASSO / Ridge** — regression on lagged values of the target plus optional exogenous predictors:
+
+    yₜ = β₀ + Σ_{i=1}^{p} βᵢ yₜ₋ᵢ + γ′xₜ + εₜ
+
+subject to an L1 (LASSO) or L2 (Ridge) penalty on the coefficients.
+*Reading:* predicts the current value from its own past (lags) and outside variables, with regularization.
+
+**Time-Series Forest / Boosting / Bagging** — tree ensembles applied to lagged features:
+
+    yₜ = f( yₜ₋₁, yₜ₋₂, …, yₜ₋ₚ, xₜ ) + εₜ
+
+where `f` is a Random Forest, Gradient Boosting, or Bagging ensemble.
+*Reading:* a flexible, non-linear function of recent history and outside predictors.
+
+**Hybrid Models (ARIMA + Machine Learning)** — the hybrid variants decompose the series into a linear part and a non-linear part:
+
+    yₜ = Lₜ + Nₜ
+    Lₜ = ARIMA/SARIMAX linear component
+    eₜ = yₜ − L̂ₜ            (the ARIMA residuals)
+    N̂ₜ = g( eₜ₋₁, …, eₜ₋ₖ )   (ML model on lagged residuals)
+    ŷₜ = L̂ₜ + N̂ₜ
+
+where `g` is LASSO, Ridge, Random Forest, Boosting, or Bagging depending on the chosen hybrid.
+*Reading:* ARIMA captures the linear trend/seasonality; a machine-learning model then learns whatever non-linear pattern is left in the residuals.
+
+---
+
+### A.3 Panel-Data Models
+
+**Fixed Effects (Within Estimator)**
+
+    yᵢₜ = αᵢ + β′xᵢₜ + εᵢₜ
+
+where `αᵢ` is a subject-specific intercept. Estimated by de-meaning within each subject:
+
+    (yᵢₜ − ȳᵢ) = β′(xᵢₜ − x̄ᵢ) + (εᵢₜ − ε̄ᵢ)
+
+*Reading:* controls for every stable, unobserved trait of each subject; identifies effects from variation *within* a subject over time.
+
+**Random Effects**
+
+    yᵢₜ = μ + β′xᵢₜ + uᵢ + εᵢₜ,   with   uᵢ ~ iid(0, σ²ᵤ)
+
+where the subject effect `uᵢ` is assumed uncorrelated with the predictors `xᵢₜ`.
+*Reading:* treats subject-level differences as random draws, using both within- and between-subject variation; more efficient than fixed effects when its assumption holds.
+
+**Panel LASSO / Ridge / Forest / Boosting / Bagging** — the regularized and tree-based panel methods use the same objective functions as their cross-sectional counterparts (Sections A.1), applied to the pooled panel `{ (yᵢₜ, xᵢₜ) }` with the subject and time columns identifying each observation.
+
+> **Note:** Symbols such as `φ`, `θ`, `β`, `λ`, and subscripts are written in plain text here so they display in any viewer. When copying into a manuscript, substitute the corresponding LaTeX (`\phi`, `\theta`, `\beta`, `\lambda`) for typeset equations.
+
